// GetUsageWithResponse Get current resource usage
//
// Current resource and billing facts across all of the caller's
// contracts. This report answers "what do I hold?" and "what is
// billable?". It does not report admission headroom. Network creation
// uses a shared client/fabric quota; prefix creation consumes a per-network
// capacity grant. Both retentions and all contracts share those technical
// limits.
//
// **Rows.** One row per `(resourceType, fabric)` pair. A row without
// `fabric` is a customer-wide aggregate. Networks have customer-wide
// and per-fabric rows; prefixes and IPv4 addresses have customer-wide
// rows, separated by address family where applicable. Only rows with
// `held > 0` are emitted. An empty report does not indicate permission to create.
//
// **Facts.** Network `held` counts networks that are neither failed nor
// deleted, including ephemeral networks and accepted provisioning
// networks. Network `persistent` counts those with persistent retention.
// VLAN presence does not affect either count. Failed networks remain
// available on the network listing for diagnostics but are excluded
// from this report. Other resource counts follow their listing selection.
// Prefix `persistent` excludes
// containers and includes eligible persistent accepted create intents.
// IPv4 address `persistent` counts role-less claims inside persistent
// prefixes; infrastructure-role addresses contribute only to `held`.
// These are resource counts, not prefix-space admission quantities.
// Failed networks do not appear here or occupy current quota.
//
// `billable` and `subscription`, when present, come from the billing
// foundation using the same selection rule as the usage export, only
// on prefix rows. This is not a historical billing time series and
// exposes no prices, currency or invoices.
//
// **Contract change.** The historical `limit` and `remaining` fields
// have been removed. They described legacy customer-wide ceilings,
// not current contract or network grants. Clients must not interpret
// their absence as unlimited capacity. Existing endpoint publication
// is retained as an explicitly approved correction; consumers of the
// removed fields must adapt before deployment (shared-api-standards
// section 10 deviation).
//
// **Filters** use the `filters[field][op]` syntax - supported:
// `filters[fabric][eq]`, `filters[resourceType][eq]`. An unknown
// filter field, or a known field with an unsupported operator,
// is a `400` (code E020013) - never
// silently ignored. More operators MAY be added additively.
//
// **No pagination.** This is a single report, not a collection:
// its cardinality is bounded (resource types × fabrics) and the
// report is only useful as one consistent snapshot - a cursor
// would split the snapshot across requests. Should the row set
// ever grow unbounded, pagination will be introduced with a new
// resource, not retrofitted here.
